Peripheral Neuropathy
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Stocking-glove sensory loss, Distal neuropathic pain, Absent Achilles reflexes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-035
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe burning pain in the feet preventing sleep, accompanied by a recent, unperceived foot injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented intractable, agonizing distal neuropathic pain causing severe sleep deprivation, complicated by the patient discovering a painless, bleeding ulcer on the sole of the foot. Urgent in-person assessment is required to provide immediate neuropathic analgesia, aggressively rule out acute diabetic foot sepsis or osteomyelitis, and initiate urgent wound care before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet burn so badly I want to put them in ice water, but today I noticed my sock was bloody and I have a hole in my heel that I can't even feel."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of a symmetrical stocking-and-glove distribution of sensory impairment. Over the last week, there has been a severe exacerbation of distal burning, tingling, and lancinating neuropathic pain, heavily pronounced at night. The patient reports a profound loss of vibratory and joint position sense in the toes. Today, the patient discovered a bleeding ulcer on the right heel. Due to the sensory loss, the trauma was completely unperceived. The patient also notes progressive distal muscle weakness, frequently presenting as a mild foot drop, and sensory ataxia resulting in a wide-based unsteady gait exacerbated in the dark. Trophic skin changes including dry anhidrotic skin are present.
Functional Status: Mobilizing independently but with high risk of falls; severe sleep impairment.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of rapidly progressive foot sepsis and potential amputation due to insensate feet.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Fatigued, visibly anxious, examining the sole of the right foot.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals the diagnostic tetrad: stocking-glove sensory loss (absent sensation to 10g monofilament and pinprick to the mid-calf bilaterally), distal neuropathic pain (hyperesthesia/allodynia to light touch on the dorsum of the feet), and early loss or marked diminution of the Achilles deep tendon reflexes (absent bilaterally). Examination of the right heel reveals a 1cm diameter, shallow neuropathic ulcer. The wound bed is pink, with mild serous exudate, but crucially, there is no surrounding erythema, purulence, or palpable fluctuance. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are bounding bilaterally. Capillary refill is brisk (<2 seconds).
Environmental Observations: Patient walking barefoot indoors; poorly lit hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Metformin 1000mg BD. Paracetamol providing zero relief for the burning pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy with Acute Neuropathic Ulcer - Status: Acute pain flare with localized tissue breakdown
Differentials:

Acute Limb Ischemia (Arterial Ulcer) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bounding peripheral pulses, warm feet, brisk capillary refill, and the ulcer located on a pressure point (heel) rather than the tips of the toes.

Acute Osteomyelitis / Deep Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, no systemic signs of toxicity, absence of spreading erythema (cellulitis), and the probe-to-bone test is completely negative in the shallow ulcer bed.

Radicular Compression (e.g., L5/S1 Herniation) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, bilateral presentation with a strict stocking-glove distribution, rather than a unilateral, sharp, shooting dermatomal pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of severe neuropathic pain complicated by a new, unperceived neuropathic foot ulcer. The combination of intractable pain preventing sleep and a newly discovered open wound in an insensate, diabetic foot prompted an urgent after-hours call. Delaying assessment would result in prolonged, severe suffering and dramatically increase the risk of the open wound rapidly converting to a limb-threatening deep space infection. Urgent after-hours assessment definitively ruled out acute limb ischemia or deep sepsis, allowed for the immediate instigation of targeted neuropathic analgesics, and provided critical on-site wound cleaning and offloading advice, fulfilling the criteria for an urgent, limb-saving intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Amitriptyline 10mg at night for neuropathic pain control. Cleansed the heel ulcer with sterile saline, applied a non-adherent dressing, and secured it firmly. Arranged an urgent, fast-tracked referral to a High-Risk Foot Clinic / Podiatry.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly wearing heavily padded, well-fitted shoes at all times (even indoors), the patient physically protects the insensate skin, immediately preventing any further traumatic puncture wounds or friction ulcers.
Step 2: By placing a mirror on the floor to visually inspect the bottom of both feet every single night, the patient bypasses their broken sensory nerves, allowing them to catch and treat minor skin breaks before they become infected holes.
Step 3: By applying a thick, unmedicated moisturizer to the top and bottom of the feet (but completely avoiding between the toes), the patient actively restores the skin barrier, preventing deep, painful cracks that invite severe infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent Podiatry/Wound Care Nurse home visit for ongoing ulcer management.
DME/Safety (Optional): Offloading heel boot or specialized therapeutic footwear.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours for wound check and review of Amitriptyline efficacy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if the foot becomes red, hot, significantly swollen, if the ulcer begins to smell bad or drain yellow pus, or if you develop a fever or severe chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Neuropathic ulcers can deteriorate rapidly; strict offloading (not walking on it) is absolutely mandatory for healing.
Clinical Course: The Amitriptyline will slowly build up to calm the burning pain over the next few days. The ulcer will take weeks to heal and requires professional podiatry care and strict pressure relief.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent medical review if the pain completely fails to respond to the new medication, or if a red streak begins traveling up your leg from the wound.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The diabetes has killed the sensory nerves in your feet. This is causing two major problems: the dying nerves are firing false "burning" pain signals, and because you can't feel pain, you injured your heel without knowing it. We are starting a medication that specifically calms those misfiring nerves, and we have dressed the wound to prevent a catastrophic infection.
Actionable Steps: Take the Amitriptyline 2 hours before bed. Never walk barefoot again. Keep the dressing completely dry and intact until you see the podiatrist or GP.

